    "problem_statement": "GPX clone method creates shallow copy instead of deep copy\n\nWhen using the `clone()` method on a GPX object, modifications to the cloned object also affect the original object. This suggests the clone method is creating a shallow copy rather than a proper deep copy.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nimport gpxpy\n\n# Load a GPX file\nwith open('test_files/cerknicko-jezero.gpx') as f:\n    gpx = gpxpy.parse(f)\n\n# Get original lengths\noriginal_2d = gpx.length_2d()\noriginal_3d = gpx.length_3d()\n\n# Clone the GPX object\ncloned_gpx = gpx.clone()\n\n# Modify the cloned object\ncloned_gpx.reduce_points(2000, min_distance=10)\ncloned_gpx.smooth(vertical=True, horizontal=True)\n\n# Check if original is affected\nprint('Original 2d length:', gpx.length_2d())\nprint('Original 3d length:', gpx.length_3d())\nprint('Expected original 2d:', original_2d)\nprint('Expected original 3d:', original_3d)\n\n# The original GPX object should remain unchanged\nassert gpx.length_2d() == original_2d  # This fails\nassert gpx.length_3d() == original_3d  # This fails\n```\n\nThe original GPX object gets modified when operations are performed on the cloned object, indicating that the clone is sharing references to the same underlying data structures."
  }
